  root.RSGAUGE = root.RSGAUGE || {};
  root.RSGAUGE.methodMd = "# METHOD — Rolling Stock Gauge Simulator\n\nThis file is **the specification**. Every equation, symbol, unit and assumption used by the simulator is\nwritten here first and then coded in `js/engine.js`. If the code and this file disagree, this file is right\nand the code is a bug.\n\nThe method is the one of the owner's gauging workbook *Vehicle Gauging Calculation Tool* (sheets Method &\nFormulas, Static Calc, Dynamic Calc, Dynamic Gauge Output). The engine reproduces that workbook to within 10⁻⁹ mm\nfor the same inputs and options; `tests/workbook.test.js` (static) and `tests/workbook-dynamic.test.js`\n(dynamic) check it on fictional inputs recalculated by the workbook.\n\n> **Status:** sections 1–8 are implemented (static: Phase 2, dynamic: Phase 3); section 10 (EN 15273-2:2009\n> kinematic method, static option) in Phase 4.\n\n## 1. Purpose and approach\n\nThe tool calculates how much space a rail vehicle needs while it moves and compares it with the customer\nkinematic envelope (KE) and with the structures along the line. The same list of movements is used in two\ndirections:\n\n* **Method B – check:** added to the static car section, it gives the **generated KE**, which must lie inside\n  the customer KE.\n* **Method A – define:** subtracted from the customer KE, it gives the **maximum static half-width** the car\n  section may have at each height.\n\n**Study direction** (Train Configuration, §7.3): *Backward* – customer KE → maximum construction gauge (define; no\nRS profile needed, the derived outline is used as the section profiles and on the route); *Forward* – RS profile →\ngenerated KE checked against the customer KE (check); *Both* (default) – the two together, with the margin of each\nRS profile to the derived gauge.\n\n**Static gauge** = car section on straight track (or a set static radius) with every movement at its limit.\n**Dynamic gauge** = the same calculation evaluated at each chainage with the local curvature, cant, speed and\nwind (Phase 3).\n\nCalculation route: Train Configuration → Car body & Bogie → Customer KE & SOD → RS Profile → Track Data →\nStatic Simulation (options, Run) → Static Results → (Dynamic Simulation → Dynamic Results) → Overall Summary.\n\n## 2. Standards and references\n\n| # | Standard / document | Used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | EN 15273-1/-2/-3 (Gauges) | Structure of the lateral and vertical reductions, symbols (a, p, n, q, w, s, hc, A) |\n| 2 | UIC 505-1 / 505-4 | Geometric overthrow formulas, yaw factor, flexibility coefficient definition |\n| 3 | Customer Schedule of Dimensions (SOD) and Employer's Requirements | Customer KE, structure gauge, curve allowances, track tolerances, load and wind cases, clearances |\n| 4 | EN 13803 | Limits of cant, cant deficiency and cant excess; equilibrium cant |\n| 5 | EN 14363 | Validation of running dynamics / multibody models |\n| 6 | EN 16586 / TSI PRM | Platform step and gap (75 mm) |\n| 7 | BOStrab / tram rules | Below about 150 m radius use the graphic or swept-path method |\n\n## 3. Conventions\n\n* Profiles are **right half outlines**: x (shown as Y in the input tables) = distance from the track centre,\n  h (Z) = height above the top of rail, entered in **mm** from the top centre clockwise down the side, as in the\n  customer drawing. The engine works in m for coordinates and mm for movements.\n* The car is checked leaning to the right (x ≥ 0); the left side is mirrored in the charts.\n* Lengths of vehicle and radii in m; plays, travels, wear, tolerances, cant and clearances in mm; speeds in\n  km/h; masses in t; stiffness in N/m and Nm/rad.\n* **Upper envelope of the KE:** the first `n_up` points (down to the cut). The notch below it (solebar,\n  collector shoe) is excluded from the car-body check. Blank `n_up` = all points.\n* **KE half-width at a height,** `W_KE(h)`: the largest x of the upper-envelope segments that span h\n  (piece-wise linear); 0 if none. In the point check h is first raised to the bottom of the upper envelope:\n  `W_KE(max(h, h_nup))`.\n* **KE top height at a half-width,** `H_KE(x)`: the largest h of the top-boundary segments (segments before the\n  widest upper-envelope point whose x increases) that span x; 0 if none.\n* **Profile half-width at a height,** `W_P(h)`: as `W_KE` over all segments of the profile.\n* **Profile top** = h of its first point.\n\n## 4. Inputs\n\n### 4.1 Train configuration\n\nStudy direction: backward / forward / both (§1, §7.3). Vehicle type (LRV / Metro / High-speed; *Apply typical values* loads `samples/vehicle-types.json`), design speed\n`Vd`, nominal track gauge `l`, distance between rail centres `G` (cant base, 1500 mm for 1435 mm gauge).\n\nPer **car type**: body length `Lb`, overhang pivot → free end `n`, overhang at a shared end `nj`, end 1 / end 2\nfree or shared (entered in the *Load definition* card: *Own bogie* or *Shared bogie*), interior section position `ni` (blank = a/2), exterior section position `na`, body width, floor\nheight `hf`, door-sill half-width `bs`, bogie type at each end, RS profile of the interior, exterior and\ncar-end sections.\n\n**Formation layout (bogie arrangement check, no physics).** Each car of the formation is placed with end 1 to the\nleft unless it is reversed. The orientation is chosen so that its left end matches the right end of the car in front\n(shared with shared, own bogie with own bogie) and no shared end is at a train end; with symmetric ends only the last\ncar is reversed (cab end outwards). Two facing shared ends form one shared bogie (articulated joint). Warnings: a\njoint with a shared end on one side and an own bogie on the other, a shared end at the front or rear of the train, a\nsingle car with a shared end, and two cars naming different bogie types for the bogie they share. The calculation\nitself is per car type (§5); the layout only checks that the car types fit together.\n\n### 4.1a Load definition (load cases and masses)\n\n**Load cases** (Train Configuration → Load definition): any number, each with a name (AW0, AW2, AW3, AW4 …) and a\nrole. Exactly one case is the **tare** (empty car: reference body height, the largest wind roll) and one the\n**crush** (fully loaded: the lowest body, used by the vertical and underframe checks of §7.2, §8.3 and the\nshared-bogie / axle-load check of §5.5); every other case has the role *other*.\n\n**Per car type and load case** (masses entered directly):\n\n| Input | Symbol | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sprung car-body mass | `m` [t] | required for every case |\n| CG height of the body above rail | `hG` [mm] | required for every case |\n| Body lowering from tare | `Δz` [mm] | tare: 0; crush: required; other: blank = interpolated |\n| Flexibility coefficient, roll-centre height | `s`, `hc` | other cases only; blank = interpolated (tare and crush values come from the car body, §4.2, or the theory, §5.4) |\n\nInterpolation of a blank value of an *other* case by its mass, between the tare (t) and crush (c) values:\n`X = X_t + (X_c − X_t)·(m − m_t)/(m_c − m_t)` (X_t when m_c = m_t).\n\n### 4.2 Car body\n\nPer car type: wind-exposed length `Lw` (blank = Lb), top `zt` and bottom `zb` of the exposed side, force\ncoefficient `Cf`; lowest body-mounted equipment height `zu` (tare, new wheels) and its half-width `yu`; lowest\nbogie-mounted equipment height `zbg`; **specified** flexibility coefficient `s` and roll-centre height `hc` for\ntare, crush and deflated. Global: air density `ρ`, base for equivalent cant `Beq` (1510 mm).\n\n### 4.3 Bogie\n\nPer bogie type: wheelbase `p`; wheel–rail half play `(l − d)/2` (worn); primary lateral play `q`; secondary\nlateral play to the bump stop `w∞` (inflated, straight track) and `wd` (deflated); secondary play on curves (§5.3a):\n`w(250)`, `w(150)` for both directions, and per direction `w_i(250)`, `w_i(150)` (towards the inside of the curve) and\n`w_a(250)`, `w_a(150)` (towards the outside), blank = the both-directions value; wheel diameter new `Dn` / worn `Dw`; maximum\nradial wear between height corrections `Δr`; vertical travels: primary downward `dp`, secondary downward\ninflated `ds` / deflated `dsd`, primary rebound `up`, secondary rebound `us`, over-inflation `uo`; bogie mass `mb`,\naxles per bogie; suspension stiffness for the theoretical s and hc (§5.4); play-calculator inputs (back-to-back\n`BB`, flange thickness new `ef` / worn `efw`).\n\nA car with two different bogie types uses the **larger** wheelbase, play and travel of the two (conservative);\nwith identical bogies this is the workbook calculation.\n\n### 4.4 Customer KE, limits and SOD\n\nKE profiles with `n_up`. Customer limits: minimum clearance of body-mounted `cb` and bogie-mounted `cbg`\nequipment to the rail, axle-load limit `P`, PRM platform gap, nosing included in the tangent KE, SOD design\nvehicle (bogie centres `C`, body length `L`, wheelbase `b`; blank = this vehicle), dynamic reference heights `h1`\n(floor / platform) and `h2` (cantrail). SOD profiles and point items are used by the dynamic outline check (§8.7).\n\n### 4.5 Track data\n\nOperating limits: maximum applied cant `E`, maximum cant deficiency `I`, maximum cant excess `Ie` (train stopped\non maximum cant), wind speed open line `Vw` and at platforms `Vwp`. Track tolerances: gauge tolerance `Δl`,\nrail gauge-face wear `fw`, alignment tolerance `al`, cross-level error `CL`, gauge widening `wd` applying below\nradius `Rw`. Static-check geometry: minimum radius `Rmin` (shared-bogie comparison), static radius `Rs`\n(0 = tangent), crest radius `Rv` for the underframe check.\n\nTrack elements (dynamic phase): chainage start / end, Tangent / Transition / Circular, hand L/R, R start / end\n(0 = ∞), cant start / end, speed limit, vertical curve Rv (+ crest, − sag), KE to use, location (Open / Tunnel /\nPlatform / Depot), wind Y/N, structure distance at h2, platform edge distance and side.\n\n### 4.6 Options (Static Simulation tab)\n\n| Option | Default | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| Customer KE | first KE | Envelope of the static check |\n| s and hc source | Specified | Specified (§4.2) or theoretical (§5.4) |\n| Theory: lateral elastic deflection in hc | Yes | Lateral term of §5.4 |\n| Suspension state – lateral / roll | Inflated | Deflated uses deflated s, hc and w |\n| Suspension state – vertical | Deflated | Secondary downward travel used in Σd |\n| Combination of random terms | Linear | Linear, or K·√Σ(random²) |\n| K (RSS) | 1 | Statistical factor |\n| Gauge tolerance in exterior / car end | No | Reference method omits it |\n| Alignment tolerance | No | Reference method lists but does not use it |\n| Rigid tilt from cross-level error | No | Adds CL/G·h |\n| Upward movement combined with roll | No | No = upward movement checked on the centre line only |\n| Wind | Yes | Wind equivalent cant |\n| Air-spring over-inflation | No | Adds `uo` to the upward movement |\n| Method A heights | 900 mm to KE top, 100 mm | Table and chart of §7 |\n| Maximum construction gauge: height step | 10 mm | Outline of §7.3 (backward and both studies), refined between the steps |\n\n## 5. Derived values per car type\n\n### 5.1 Geometry\n\n* End overhangs: `n1 = nj` if end 1 is shared, else `n`; `n2` likewise.\n* Pivot distance: `a = Lb − n1 − n2`.\n* Largest overhang (car-end section): `nE = max(n1, n2)`; check `na ≤ nE`.\n* Yaw factor beyond the pivot: `A = (a + 2n)/a` → `A_ext` with n = na, `A_end` with n = nE; between pivots A = 1.\n\n### 5.2 Wind as equivalent cant\n\n* `Aw = Lw·(zt − zb)`, `F = ½·ρ·Cf·Aw·(V/3.6)²` [N].\n* `Cw = ½·ρ·Cf·Aw·Beq / (m·g) / 3.6²` [mm/(km/h)²] for the sprung mass m of each load case (tare `Cw0`,\n  crush `Cw4`), so that `D = Cw·V²` [mm].\n* `g = 9.81 m/s²`.\n\n### 5.3 Vertical travels\n\n* Radial wheel wear used: `Δw = min((Dn − Dw)/2, Δr)`.\n* Total downward movement (lower parts): `Σd = Δw + dp + (dsd if vertical state deflated, else ds)`.\n* Total upward movement (upper parts): `Σu = up + us + (uo if over-inflation selected)`.\n* Secondary lateral play used: `w* = wd` if the lateral state is deflated, else `w`.\n\n### 5.3a Secondary clearance varying with the radius: w∞, w_i(R), w_a(R)\n\nWith rotation stops or lateral stops that act in curves, the clearance between bogie and body depends on the radius\nand on the direction: `w∞` on straight track, `w_i(R)` towards the inside of the curve, `w_a(R)` towards the outside.\n\n* Per bogie and direction: `w(R) = w∞ + (w(250) − w∞)·(250/R)` for `R ≥ 250 m`;\n  `w(R) = w(250) + (w(150) − w(250))·(1/R − 1/250)/(1/150 − 1/250)` for `150 ≤ R < 250 m`; `w(150)` below 150 m\n  (linear in 1/R, as EN 15273-2 interpolates between its rule radii). Blanks: `w_i(250)` → `w(250)` → `w∞`;\n  `w_i(150)` → `w_i(250)` when only that is given and `w(150)` is blank, else `w(150)`; the same for `w_a`.\n* A car uses the larger value of its two bogies in each direction (conservative, as §4.3).\n* Deflated lateral state: `wd` in both directions and at every radius.\n* Where it is used: the workbook method at the static radius `Rs` (§6.1), the route sweep at the governing radius\n  (§8.3) and the EN 15273-2 reductions (§10.4). Variable stops (§10.6) remain an EN-only input.\n\n### 5.3b Rotation stops\n\nOptional per bogie type: stops between bogie and body at a distance `x` ahead of and behind the bogie centre, on both\nsides, with a free lateral gap `g` (body centred, straight track) and an elastic travel `t` of the stop face.\n\n* Bogie yaw relative to the body on a curve of radius R: `ψ = asin(a/(2R))` (the body chord between the pivots against\n  the bogie, aligned with the track at its centre). A stop at `x` closes by `ψ·x` – the front stop on one side and the\n  rear stop on the other side, i.e. a **diagonal pair**.\n* **Free rotation** (design rules: the stop must not obstruct the rotation; no simultaneous contact on two diagonally\n  opposite stops): at the smallest radius `Rmin` (the smaller of the track parameter *Rmin* and the smallest radius in\n  the Track Data) `margin = g − ψ(Rmin)·x ≥ 0`; `g + t − ψ·x` = margin to a solid stop (information).\n* **Contact in service (comfort):** the body drifts on the secondary springs by\n  `y_q = (m/2)·a_q / (ψe·na·ky2)` (heaviest load case, `a_q = g·(I + Dw)/G` with the largest cant deficiency `I` and the\n  wind equivalent cant `Dw` if wind is on; `ψe` = 1 own bogie, ½ shared; at most `g`). A stop touches below the radius\n  `R_c = a / (2·sin((g − y_q)/x))` (rotation only: `y_q = 0`). The tool gives the length and share of the line with\n  `R < R_c`; when a comfort radius is entered, `R_c ≤ R_comf` is required (no contact on curves of `R_comf` and larger).\n* **Limit on w:** with the stops, the lateral clearance left at the pivot in either direction is\n  `w_rs(R) = g + t − ψ(R)·x` (front and rear stops of one side). With *Rotation stops limit w(R)* = Yes,\n  `w_i(R) = min(w_i entered, w_rs(R))`, `w_a(R) = min(w_a entered, w_rs(R))` and `w∞ = min(w∞, g + t)` in every\n  method (§5.3a, §6.1, §8.3, §10.4). With No, a warning is given when the entered w is larger than `w_rs`.\n* Free rotation, and the comfort radius when entered, are part of the car type's verdict.\n\n### 5.3c Further vertical displacements: body camber / sag, end deflection, pitch, track unevenness, rail head wear\n\nOptional, all 0 by default (the reference workbook has none of them). Car body: pre-camber `fc` (upward bow at mid-car,\ntare), sag `fs` (downward at mid-car, crush against tare), deflection at the car end upward `eu` / downward `ed`, pitch\n`Δzp` (height difference between the two pivots: different suspension state or wheel wear of the two bogies). Track:\nvertical unevenness `uv` (longitudinal level), vertical rail head wear `rw`.\n\n* Between the pivots, at `n` from a pivot, shape `σ = 4·n·(a − n)/a²` (1 at mid-car):\n  upward `fc·σ + uv`, downward `fs·σ + uv + rw`.\n* Beyond a pivot, at `n`, `ρ = n/nE` (1 at the car end): upward `eu·ρ + Δzp·n/a + uv`,\n  downward `ed·ρ + Δzp·n/a + uv + rw` (pitch about the far pivot: the overhang rises at one end and falls at the other).\n* Use: profile top + Σu + upward addition of the section ≤ KE top (§7.2); with *Upward movement combined with roll*\n  the addition is in `up` of the case (§6.1); body-mounted equipment `zu − Σd − vc − θ·yu − downward addition at\n  mid-car ≥ cb`; bogie-mounted equipment loses `uv + rw`; the gauge outline top and bottom per section (§7.3); the\n  route underframe height (§8.3).\n* EN 15273-2 heights (§10.7) keep their own terms (Table A.1); these inputs are not added there.\n\n### 5.3d Secondary suspension travel with sag and roll\n\nThe suspension deflections and the body roll may be added as in §6 only while the total deflection stays within the\nsuspension's travel. Optional per bogie type: free vertical travel of the secondary to the bump stop (downward, from\ntare) `zb` and to the rebound stop (upward, from tare) `zr`; blank = no check.\n\n* Per load case: the largest suspension roll of its cases `θ = θq + θr` (rigid tilt not included); the secondary's\n  share from the theoretical stiffness model of that load (§5.4), `θ2 = θ·Kφ/Kφ2` (springs in series: the roll splits\n  in the inverse ratio of the stiffnesses; 1 when no stiffness is given); deflection at the air spring `θ2·b2`.\n* Low side: `zb − Δz − θ2·b2 ≥ 0` (Δz = body lowering of the load case); high side: `zr + Δz − θ2·b2 ≥ 0`.\n* Not checked in the deflated state (emergency springs). Part of the car type's verdict (\"ON THE STOP\" = the linear\n  sum of §6 is no longer valid: the roll is limited by the stop and the gauge must be checked with the stop forces).\n\n### 5.3e Electrical clearance to the contact line (rigid catenary)\n\nLive parts of a rigid catenary / contact line are entered as SOD items of type *Overhead line* (y, z, side, chainage\nrange). Customer limits: static clearance (default 290 mm) and dynamic clearance (default 190 mm), the prescribed\nminimum clearances between live parts and earthed parts of the vehicle.\n\n* **Static:** car at rest, centred on straight track: the distance from each live point (|y|, z) to every section\n  profile of the run (signed distance to the mirrored outline, + outside) ≥ the static value. Part of the verdict.\n* **Dynamic:** along the line the item check of §8.7 uses for *Overhead line* items the larger of the item's own\n  required clearance and the dynamic value.\n* The pantograph itself is live and is checked by its own gauge (§10.11); this check covers the earthed roof equipment.\n\n### 5.4 Theoretical flexibility coefficient and roll centre\n\nRoll-stiffness share per body end: ψ = 1 for an own bogie, ½ for a shared bogie. With ψ1, ψ2 of the two ends:\n\n* Primary roll stiffness: `Kφ1 = Σ ψ·np·kz1·b1²`\n* Secondary roll stiffness: `Kφ2 = Σ ψ·(na·kz2·b2² + kφr)` with kz2 = tare / crush air-spring stiffness or the\n  emergency-spring stiffness when deflated; an *other* load case uses kz2 interpolated by its mass between the tare\n  and crush stiffness (§4.1a). The deflated state uses the tare mass and CG.\n* In series: `Kφ = 1/(1/Kφ1 + 1/Kφ2)`; lateral: `Ky = 1/(1/Σψ·np·ky1 + 1/Σψ·na·ky2)` (ky2d when deflated;\n  crush uses the tare lateral stiffness).\n* Weights: `w1 = (hG − zp)/Kφ1`, `w2 = (hG − zs)/Kφ2` (zs deflated when deflated).\n* Roll centre: `hc,0 = (w1·zp + w2·zs)/(w1 + w2)`; `hc = hc,0 − (1/Ky)/(w1 + w2)` if the lateral term is selected.\n* Flexibility: `s = m·g·(hG − hc) / (Kφ − m·g·(hG − hc))` (from Kφ·η = m·g·h′·(δ + η), s = η/δ).\n* Plausibility: s < 0 or s > 0.6 → \"REVIEW – very soft in roll\"; hc < 0.1 m → \"REVIEW – low hc\".\n* States: tare (m0, hG0, kz2 tare), crush (m4, hG4, kz2 crush), each other load case (m, hG, kz2 interpolated),\n  deflated (m0, hG0, emergency spring).\n\nValues used: lateral state deflated → the deflated s and hc for every load case; otherwise, per load case, the\nchosen source (specified: car body tare / crush values, an *other* case its own or interpolated values, §4.1a;\ntheoretical: the state of that load case).\n\n### 5.5 Shared-bogie comparison and axle load (heaviest load case)\n\n* Pivot reactions: `R2 = m4·(Lb/2 − n1)/a`, `R1 = m4 − R2` (body CG at mid-length), with `m4` the largest sprung\n  mass of the load cases (normally the crush case).\n* Bogie loads: `Q1 = (2·R1 if end 1 shared, else R1) + mb1`, `Q2` likewise (identical neighbour assumed).\n* Axle load: `P = max(Q1/axles1, Q2/axles2)`, checked against the limit.\n* Throws at `Rmin`: mid `(a² + p²)/(8R)`, end `(a·nE + nE² − p²/4)/(2R)`; the same for the non-shared reference\n  with `a_ref = Lb − 2n` and overhang n. Width lost (both sides) = `2·max(Δmid, Δend, 0)`.\n* Largest pivot distance for the non-shared mid-throw: `a = √(8R·throw − p²)`.\n\n### 5.6 Play calculator (information)\n\n`l = nominal gauge + Δl`, `d = BB + 2·ef` (new), `BB + 2·efw` (worn); half play `(l − d)/2`.\n\n## 6. Static calculation – Method B (check)\n\n### 6.1 Cases\n\nThree sections per car type: interior (between pivots, at `ni`), exterior (beyond the pivot, at `na`) and car end\n(at `nE`), each with every load case (§4.1a, in the order of the load-case table; with AW0 and AW4 the six cases of\nthe reference workbook). Interior = **train stopped** on maximum cant (cant excess `Ie`); exterior and car\nend = **train at speed** (cant deficiency `I`).\n\nCase parameters:\n\n| Quantity | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Geometric overthrow at Rs | interior `dgi = (a·n − n² + p²/4)/(2Rs)`; exterior / end `dga = (a·n + n² − p²/4)/(2Rs)`; 0 on tangent |\n| Yaw factor | 1 (interior), `A_ext`, `A_end` |\n| Gauge widening | `wd/2` if 0 < Rs < Rw, else 0 |\n| Secondary play term | tangent (`Rs = 0`) or no curve values: `w*·A`; on a curve: interior `w_i(Rs)`, exterior / end `w_i(Rs)·n/a + w_a(Rs)·(n + a)/a` (§5.3a; the body yaws towards the inside at the far bogie and towards the outside at the near bogie; equal values give `w·A`) |\n| Quasi-static translation | `T = dg + A·[(l − d)/2 + q + fw + wd/2] + secondary play term` |\n| Random: gauge tolerance | interior `Δl`; exterior / end `Δl·A` only if selected |\n| Random: alignment | `al` if selected |\n| Cant | `Ie` (interior), `I` (exterior, end) |\n| Wind equivalent cant | `Cw(load)·Vw²` if wind selected |\n| Roll angle quasi-static | `θq = s·(cant + wind)/G` |\n| Roll angle cross-level (random) | `θr = s·CL/G` |\n| Rigid tilt (random) | `θt = CL/G` if selected |\n| Total angle for vertical shift | `θ = θq + θr + θt` |\n| Body lowering | `Δz` of the load case (0 for tare, §4.1a) |\n| Upward movement with roll | `Σu` if selected, else 0 |\n\n### 6.1a Additional sections\n\nOptional, per car type (Train Configuration → *Additional sections*): name, reference end (end 1 or end 2), distance\n`d` from that body end [m] and the RS profile of the section. Position from body end 1: `x = d` (end 1) or\n`x = Lb − d` (end 2), `0 ≤ x ≤ Lb`; the pivots are at `n1` and `Lb − n2` (§5.1).\n\n* **Between the pivots** (`n1 ≤ x ≤ Lb − n2`): the interior rules of §6.1 with `n = x − n1` (the overthrow\n  `a·n − n²` is the same from either pivot), `A = 1`, cant excess `Ie`, gauge tolerance `Δl`.\n* **Beyond a pivot**: the exterior rules of §6.1 with `n = n1 − x` (end 1 side) or `n = x − (Lb − n2)` (end 2 side),\n  `A = (a + 2n)/a`, cant deficiency `I`, gauge tolerance `Δl·A` only if selected.\n\nEach additional section is checked for every load case (Method B, §6.4; it takes part in the governing case), gets its\nown Method A column (§7.1) and a profile-top row in the vertical checks (§7.2). It is not part of the EN 15273-2\nchecks (§10), the BOStrab check (§16) or the dynamic sweep (§8).\n\n### 6.2 Lateral movement at height h (mm)\n\n* Linear: `E(h) = T + θq·(h − hc) + Δl* + al* + θr·(h − hc) + θt·h`\n* Statistical: `E(h) = T + θq·(h − hc) + K·√(Δl*² + al*² + (θr·(h − hc))² + (θt·h)²)`\n\n(h, hc in mm here; `Δl*`, `al*` are the random terms of §6.1.)\n\n### 6.3 Displaced point\n\nFor each point (x, h) of the section profile:\n\n`x′ = x + E(h)`, `h′ = h − θ·x − Δz + up`\n\n### 6.4 Clearance of a point to the KE\n\n* `ΔX = W_KE(max(h′, h_nup)) − x′`, `ΔH = H_KE(x′) − h′`\n* Both ≥ 0: `c = ΔX·ΔH / √(ΔX² + ΔH²)` (0 if either is 0); both < 0: `c = −ΔX·ΔH / √(ΔX² + ΔH²)`;\n  otherwise `c = min(ΔX, ΔH)`.\n* `c ≥ 0` → OK (inside); `c < 0` → INFRINGES. Exact for a straight KE segment, conservative near corners.\n* Case result: the minimum c over the points (first point if equal). Car result: the minimum over all\n  cases (3 sections × the load cases); run result: the minimum over the car types checked (governing car and case).\n\n## 7. Method A (define) and vertical checks\n\n### 7.1 Maximum static half-width\n\nAt each height h of the Method A grid (default 900 mm to the KE top in 100 mm steps, last height clipped to\nthe KE top):\n\n* `x_max,int(h) = W_KE(h) − max over the load cases of E_int,load(h)`, likewise exterior and car end.\n* Margin to the section profile: `x_max − W_P(h)`; blank above the profile top (W_P = 0).\n* Vertical shift is ignored (horizontal margins only); near the roof shoulder Method B governs.\n\n### 7.2 Vertical checks\n\n| Check | Value | Limit |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interior / exterior / car-end profile top + Σu (centre line) | `top + Σu` | KE top |\n| Vertical-curve lowering at mid-car (crest Rv) | `a²/(8Rv)` | information |\n| Roll lowering of the underframe edge | `max θ of the crush cases · yu` | information |\n| Body-mounted equipment height, worst case | `zu − Σd − a²/(8Rv) − θ·yu` | ≥ cb |\n| Bogie-mounted equipment height, worst case | `zbg − Δw − dp` | ≥ cbg |\n\n### 7.3 Maximum construction gauge outline (backward study)\n\nThe full inverse of Method B: the largest closed cross-section whose every point, moved by every movement of every\ncase of the section (§6.1–§6.3: `x′ = x + E(h)`, `h′ = h − θ·x − Δz + up`), stays inside the customer KE (§6.4,\n`c ≥ 0`). Calculated for the interior, exterior and car-end sections, each additional section (§6.1a) and the\n**whole car** (all cases of the three standard sections together = a constant-section car body).\n\n* **Side:** at each height h of a grid (step `Δh`, default 10 mm, from the top downwards) the largest half-width\n  `x_max(h)` with `c ≥ 0` in every case; where the displaced point is below the bottom of the KE's upper envelope it\n  must also lie inside the whole KE outline (signed distance ≥ 0 to the mirrored outline – a third-rail or platform\n  notch of the lower part; Method B itself does not look there): scan in 10 mm steps from the centre line, then bisection (40 halvings).\n  Between two grid heights the straight line of the outline is checked at the mid-height; where it lies outside the\n  true `x_max`, the mid-height point is added (up to 6 levels, 0,16 mm with the default step).\n* **Top:** `h_top = H_KE,top − Σu` on the centre line (§7.2; with *Upward movement combined with roll* = Yes the\n  upward movement is already in `h′` and `h_top = H_KE,top`); lower if even `x = 0` leaves the KE (bisection).\n* **Bottom:** on the centre line `z_b = cb + Σd + a²/(8Rv)` (§7.2, Rv static crest; 0 on level track), rising\n  towards the side with the roll of the crush cases: `z(y) = z_b + θ_crush·y`. The side ends where this line meets\n  `x_max`. Lower parts below the upper envelope of the KE are not part of Method B; check them with §7.2 / §10.7.\n* **Points** from the top centre round the side to the bottom centre (`M1 … Mn`, mm): y rounded down to 0,1 mm and\n  pulled in by 0,1 mm steps if a vertex would leave the KE after rounding; points whose neighbours' chord stays on or\n  inside them (within 0,05 mm) are dropped.\n* **Use by study:** *backward* – the section outlines are the RS profiles of §6 (Method B then closes at about 0 mm, a\n  self-check), the EN / BOStrab checks see the derived gauge, and the dynamic sweep (§8) uses the whole-car outline;\n  *both* – the RS profiles are checked as usual and each gets a horizontal margin to its section outline\n  (`x_max(h) − x_prof(h)` at the profile vertices inside the gauge's height range) and a top margin\n  (`h_top − profile top`); *forward* – not calculated.\n* The outline is a check result, not a design: it does not include the equipment, door or pantograph rules of §10\n  and assumes the plays and suspension of the inputs.\n\n## 8. Dynamic calculation (chainage sweep)\n\n### 8.1 Track model and sweep\n\n* Elements sorted by start chainage; signed curvature at start and end `k = ±1/R` (+ left, − right, 0 for R = 0).\n* Sweep chainages: `ch0, ch0 + Δx, …` while ≤ the end of the track data (`Δx` = sweep step on the Track Data\n  tab; start / end chainage options, blank = whole line). The end chainage itself is also evaluated and\n  flagged *not valid*: it enters the maxima and minima but not the point counts (the workbook's repeated end rows).\n* At chainage `x`: the element is the last one whose start is ≤ x; position `t = (x − s0)/(s1 − s0)` clamped to\n  0…1; `k = ks + (ke − ks)·t`, cant `E = Es + (Ee − Es)·t` (linear in transitions); speed limit, Rv, KE,\n  location, wind, structure and platform data of the element (blank KE = the KE of the static check).\n\n### 8.2 Constants per car type\n\n* Load case for the dynamic calculation (option, any load case; the tare case by default): `s`, `hc`, `Cw` of that\n  load (§4.1a, §5.4 rules for the suspension state).\n* `dT_in = (l−d)/2 + q + w* + fw`, `dT_out = A_end·dT_in`.\n* Random terms at height h: interior `Δl + al* + θr·(h − hc) + θt·h` (or K·√Σ²), exterior with `Δl·A_end`\n  only if gauge tolerance in the exterior is selected; `θr = s·CL/G`, `θt = CL/G` if selected.\n* Profile for the dynamic gauge: the option, blank = car-end section profile of the car type; its half-widths\n  `x_prof(h1)`, `x_prof(h2)` (all segments, §3).\n* Window for the governing curvature: `ROUNDUP((a/2 + nE + p/2)/Δx)` sweep points each side.\n* SOD design vehicle: `C`, `L`, `b` from the customer limits, blank = this car type's `a`, `Lb`, `p`.\n\n### 8.3 At each sweep point\n\n| Quantity | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Governing curvature | local `|k|`, or (conservative, default) the largest `|k|` within ± window points |\n| Governing radius | `Rg = 1/|k_gov|` (0 = straight) |\n| Equilibrium cant | `Eeq = G·(V/3.6)²·|k|/g` (local curvature, speed limit of the element) |\n| Cant deficiency / excess | `I = max(Eeq − E, 0)` at line speed; excess = E (train stopped) |\n| Wind speed | 0 if wind N; platform → `Vwp`; else `Vw` |\n| Wind equivalent cant | `D = Cw·Vwind²` (if wind selected) |\n| Gauge widening | `wd` if 0 < Rg < Rw |\n| Overthrow | `dgi = (a² + p²)/8·|k_gov|`, `dga = (a·nE + nE² − p²/4)/2·|k_gov|` |\n| Plays at the governing radius Rg | `dT_in = (l − d)/2 + q + fw + w_i(Rg)`; `dT_out = A_end·[(l − d)/2 + q + fw] + w_i(Rg)·nE/a + w_a(Rg)·(nE + a)/a` (§5.3a; straight track or no curve values: `dT_out = A_end·dT_in` with `w*`) |\n| Required half-width OUTSIDE at h | `x_prof(h) + dga + dT_out + A_end·wd/2 + s·(I + D)/G·(h − hc) + random_out(h)` |\n| Required half-width INSIDE at h | `x_prof(h) + dgi + dT_in + wd/2 + s·(E + D)/G·(h − hc) + random_in(h)` |\n| KE half-width | `W_KE(h1)`, `W_KE(h2)` of the element's KE (upper envelope) |\n| Excess over the tangent KE | OUT `max(OUT(h1) − W_KE(h1), OUT(h2) − W_KE(h2))`, IN likewise |\n| SOD extra allowance | `T_out = max((L² − C²)/8·|k_gov| − nosing, 0) + wd/2`; `T_in = max((C² + b²)/8·|k_gov| − nosing, 0) + wd/2` |\n| SOD check | OK if excess OUT ≤ T_out + 0.05 and excess IN ≤ T_in + 0.05 |\n| Lean due to cant | `h2·E/G` |\n| Structure clearance at h2 | `S − max(OUT(h2) − lean, IN(h2) + lean)` (S = structure distance; none if 0) |\n| Vertical-curve effect | crest `a²/(8Rv)`, sag `(a·nE + nE²)/(2|Rv|)` |\n| Underframe height | `zu − Σd − vc − s_crush·(max(I, E) + Cw_crush·Vwind² + CL)/G·yu` ≥ cb |\n| Platform position | at a platform: tangent if k = 0; inside if (side L and k > 0) or (side R and k < 0); else outside |\n| Platform gap nominal | `PE − bs − (dgi inside, dga outside, 0 tangent)` |\n| Platform gap minimum | `nominal − (dT_in + wd/2 + s·((E if inside) + D)/G·(hf − hc) + random_in(hf))`; < 0 → CONTACT; nominal > PRM → information |\n| Overall | CHECK if SOD check, structure, underframe or platform fails |\n\n### 8.4 Summaries\n\n* Line summary: length, points, maximum deficiency (vs the limit), maximum excess OUT / IN, points above the SOD\n  allowance, minimum structure clearance, minimum underframe height, minimum and maximum nominal platform gap,\n  points with any CHECK; each with its chainage (first occurrence).\n* Per element: maximum deficiency, OUT(h2), IN(h2), excess OUT / IN; minimum structure clearance and underframe\n  height; status (not evaluated if no sweep point falls in the element).\n* All car types: each car type is swept; the line summary takes the worst car type at each chainage (largest\n  excesses and nominal gap, smallest clearances; CHECK if any car type checks), and names the governing car.\n\n### 8.5 Speed-step study\n\nRadius `R` (0 = tangent), cant `E`, wind speed and KE set in the options; speeds `0, ΔV, …` (13 steps by default):\n`Eeq = G·(V/3.6)²/(g·R)`, `I = Eeq − E` (deficiency = max(I, 0), excess = max(−I, 0)), overthrow at R, the OUT / IN\nhalf-widths of §8.3 at h1, h2, the KE half-widths and the maximum excess; remarks above the cant-deficiency limit\nor above the design speed.\n\n### 8.6 Cross-section at a chainage\n\nSweep point at or before the chainage (blank = largest outside excess). For each profile point: right side\n(OUTSIDE) lateral `dga + dT_out + A_end·wd/2 + s·(I + D)/G·(h − hc) + random_out(h)` and roll\n`θo = s·(I + D)/G + θr + θt`; left side (INSIDE) with dgi, dT_in, wd/2, E and `θi`. Track-plane point\n`(±(x + E_lat), h − θ·x)`, rotated by `φ = asin(E/G)` into the vertical frame:\n`X = x·cos φ − z·sin φ`, `Z = x·sin φ + z·cos φ`. The static profile, the KE and the KE + SOD allowance (x + T_out on\nthe right, x + T_in on the left) are rotated the same way; rails at ±750 mm.\n\n### 8.7 SOD outline and point items\n\nAt every sweep point and for every car type, the whole dynamic-gauge profile (§8.2) is displaced as in §8.6:\nOUTSIDE points (right in the curve frame) `(x + E_out(h), h − θo·x)`, INSIDE points (left) `(−(x + E_in(h)), h − θi·x)`;\nthe vehicle envelope is the closed polygon of these points. Frame (Customer KE & SOD tab): *track plane* — compared as\nthey are; *vertical* — envelope rotated by `φ = asin(E/G)` (§8.6).\n\n**SOD outline.** The SOD profile named on the track element (blank = no outline check) is a half outline (Y ≥ 0),\nmirrored. It is the tangent SOD: on curves it is widened laterally by the SOD allowance of §8.3, `y + T_out` on the\noutside and `y + T_in` on the inside. Clearance of each envelope point = signed shortest distance to the widened outline,\npositive inside the free space. Required: clearance ≥ the minimum clearance KE to SOD (Customer KE & SOD tab). The\nsmallest clearance and its point are kept per sweep point.\n\n**Point items.** Each item has Y, Z, a side (left, right, both; left / right in the direction of increasing chainage) and\nan optional chainage range (blank = whole line); it is checked where the sweep chainage is within its range. Real side to\ncurve frame: left-hand curve (k > 0) → right is the outside; right-hand curve → left is the outside; tangent (k = 0) → both\nhands are checked. Clearance = signed shortest distance from the item to the vehicle envelope, positive outside it;\nmargin = clearance − the item's required clearance ≥ 0. The smallest margin and its item are kept per sweep point.\n\nBoth checks enter the overall CHECK of the sweep point (§8.3), the line and element summaries (§8.4, smallest outline\nclearance and item margin, points failing) and the worst-car envelope. Distances are true Euclidean distances to the\npolygon edges (not the horizontal / vertical split of §6.4).\n\n## 9. Verification\n\n1. **Workbook reproduction** (`tests/workbook.test.js`): fictional inputs were entered in the reference workbook\n   and recalculated (LibreOffice); for several option sets (linear / RSS, deflated, theoretical s and hc, gauge\n   and alignment tolerance, rigid tilt, upward movement with roll, static curve with widening, shared bogies) the\n   engine matches every case minimum, governing point, Method A row and vertical check.\n2. **Dynamic reproduction** (`tests/workbook-dynamic.test.js`): a fictional 4.4 km line with transitions,\n   vertical curves, platforms, tunnels and a depot curve, swept in the workbook for four option sets (conservative\n   / local overthrow, tare / crush, RSS, deflated, tolerances and tilt, theoretical s and hc, no wind): every\n   column of every sweep row, the line summary, the per-element table, the speed-step study and the cross-section\n   outline match.\n3. **SOD outline and items** (`tests/sod-outline.test.js`): closed-form cases (rectangular outline on tangent, curve\n   widening, cant rotation in the vertical frame, item sides and ranges, curve hand).\n4. Closed-form checks: tangent with no movement gives clearance = gap to the KE; yaw factor; wind constant;\n   theoretical s from the closed form; axle load by statics.\n5. **EN 15273-2** (`tests/en15273.test.js`): reductions and z_kin against the standard's formulae A.24, A.27–A.31\n   and A.46–A.50 typed in literally, 40 000 random vehicles, both branches; a hand check. Heights of A.3.4\n   (`tests/en15273-vertical.test.js`): Table A.1 zones and A.16–A.19 typed in literally, hand checks of the sample.\n   Bogie parts (`tests/en15273-bogie.test.js`): A.56–A.59 typed in literally, hand check of the sample.\n   Static gauge (`tests/en15273-static.test.js`): A.2–A.5 typed in literally, s limit, hand check of the sample.\n   Doors and steps (`tests/en15273-doors.test.js`): A.105 / A.106 typed in, step gaps by hand.\n   Pantographs (`tests/en15273-panto.test.js`): A.66–A.85 typed in literally, hand check.\n   Other gauges (`tests/en15273-gauges.test.js`): GA / GB rules B.1–B.10, B.21–B.26 and the DE2 roll term typed in.\n6. Inputs are never mutated; results are deterministic.\n7. **Third-rail CCD** (`tests/ccd.test.js`): movements, reach, overlap, envelope and clearance by hand for bottom, top and\n   side contact; static situations and the line sweep on the sample.\n8. **EN dynamic method** (`tests/en-dynamic.test.js`): O.4–O.18 by hand, rigid-body interpolation, curving, case choice, O.6\n   import, validation limits of Tables O.1–O.3 and the status of Figure O.1.\n9. **Framework and BOStrab §34** (`tests/framework.test.js`): defaults per framework, §34 widths, height and curve throw by hand.\n\n## 10. EN 15273-2:2009 kinematic method (static option)\n\nOptional check on the Static Simulation tab (*Also check EN 15273-2:2009 kinematic*). The **customer KE is the\nreference profile**; its associated rules are the parameters on the Customer KE & SOD tab, with the values of gauge\nG1 (EN 15273-2:2009 Annex A.3) as defaults. Implemented from the 2009 edition (superseded by EN 15273-2:2013; to be\nupdated when that edition is used). §5.1.1: the half-width of the vehicle shall not exceed the half-width of the\nreference profile reduced by `Ei` (sections between the bogie centres) or `Ea` (sections beyond them).\n\n### 10.1 Rules (Customer KE & SOD tab, G1 values)\n\n| Symbol | Meaning | G1 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `l_max` | maximum track gauge of the rules | 1,465 m |\n| `e0` | lateral allowance included in the reference profile | 0,015 m |\n| `R1`, `R2` | reference radii | 250 m, 150 m |\n| `Ck` | overthrow allowance `k = Ck/R` – lower parts / upper parts | 2,5 / 3,75 m² |\n| `Xi`, `Xa` | x-term constants between / beyond the bogie centres | 100 / 120 m² |\n| `h_low` | lower parts: up to this height above the rail | 0,400 m |\n| `s0`, `hc0` | reference flexibility and roll-centre height | 0,4; 0,5 m |\n| `I0`, `D0` | cant excess (inside) / deficiency (outside) in the reference profile | 0,05 m |\n| `Dlim` | cant excess / deficiency limit | 0,2 m |\n| `L` | distance between rail centres of the rules | 1,5 m |\n| `η_lim` | dissymmetry included in the reference profile | 1° |\n\n### 10.2 Vehicle data\n\n* Formula set (Table A.4): no motor bogie → coach formulae A.3.9.3; two motor bogies (bogie input *Motor bogie*,\n  adhesion μ ≥ 0,2) → traction-unit formulae A.3.9.1; one motor bogie → traction-unit formulae by default (note a of\n  Table A.4) or the motor-coach formulae A.3.9.2 (Static Simulation option).\n* `a` pivot distance; per bogie: wheelbase `p`, secondary lateral clearance `w∞` (straight track; deflated `w` when the\n  lateral state is deflated), `w(250)` and `w(150)` (blank = constant), and, for variable stops, the radius `Rv` where\n  they start with `w(Rv)`. `d` = BB + 2·ef worn (smaller of the two bogies: largest play), `q` larger of the two.\n* Sections: interior at `n = ni` (between the bogie centres), exterior at `n = na` and car end at `n = n_end` (beyond),\n  with the profiles of §6. `s`, `hc` of every load case (§4.1a, §5.4 source and suspension state); `E` is the largest of them.\n  For a section beyond a pivot the bogie at that end (`a`, weight `(n + a)/a`) and the far bogie (`i`, weight `n/a`) can\n  be either bogie of the car: both orientations are evaluated and the larger `E` is used.\n* `η0`: body dissymmetry (car body input, default 0).\n\n### 10.3 Quasi-static term z_kin (A.3.8.1, A.24)\n\nWith `C0 = I0` for `Ei` and `C0 = D0` for `Ea`, `k2 = (Dlim − C0)/L`:\n\n`z = s·C0/L·(h − hc)>0 + tan(η0 − η_lim)>0·|h − hc| + [ s·k2·(h − hc)>0 − s0·k2·(h − hc0)>0 ]>0`\n\nSpecial cases (η0 ≤ η_lim): `z = 0` for `h < hc0`; `z = s·C0/L·(h − hc)>0` for `s ≤ s0`. With the G1 values this is\n`s/30·(h − hc) + [s/10·(h − hc) − 0,04·(h − 0,5)]` of A.24. Unsprung parts (z = 0) are not distinguished: every\nprofile point is treated as part of the body.\n\n### 10.4 Reductions – traction units and coaches (A.3.9.1.1, A.3.9.3.1)\n\n`Δi = a·n − n² + p²/4`, `Δa = a·n + n² − p²/4` (p of the bogie at the section's end), `A = (2n + a)/a`, `P = (l_max − d)/2`,\n`fa = (n + a)/a`, `fi = n/a`; `Ck` of the lower parts for `h ≤ h_low`, else of the upper parts; `k1 = 1/(2·R1)`,\n`kx = 1/(2·R2) − 1/(2·R1)`. Between the pivots `w` is that of one bogie (both bogies are evaluated); beyond them\n`w_i` (far bogie) and `w_a` (bogie at the end):\n\n| | condition for the straight branch | straight branch | curve branch |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Traction Ei (A.27–A.29) | `Δi − 2R1·(w∞ − w(250)) ≤ 2Ck` | `P + q + w∞ + z − e0` | `Δi·k1 + P + q + w(250) + z + [x]>0 − e0 − Ck/R1` |\n| Traction Ea (A.30–A.32) | `Δa − 2R1·W ≤ 2Ck` | `(P + q)·A + w∞i·fi + w∞a·fa + z − e0` | `Δa·k1 + (P + q)·A + w_i(250)·fi + w_a(250)·fa + z + [x]>0 − e0 − Ck/R1` |\n| Coach Ei (A.46–A.48) | `Δi − 2R1·(w∞ − w(250)) ≤ R1·(l_max − d) − 2·(Ck_up − Ck)` | `P + q + w∞ + z − e0` | `Δi·k1 + q + w(250) + z + [x]>0 − Ck/R1` |\n| Coach Ea (A.49–A.51) | `Δa − 2R1·W ≤ R1·(l_max − d)·fi + 2Ck` | `(P + q)·A + w∞i·fi + w∞a·fa + z − e0` | `Δa·k1 + P·fa + q·A + w_i(250)·fi + w_a(250)·fa + z + [x]>0 − e0 − Ck/R1` |\n\nwith `W = (w∞i − w_i(250))·fi + (w∞a − w_a(250))·fa`. Directional clearances (§5.3a): between the pivots the body moves\ntowards the inside of the curve at the bogie (`w = w_i(R)` of that bogie); beyond them `w_i` is the inside value of the\nfar bogie and `w_a` the outside value of the bogie at the section's end. Without directional inputs both are `w(R)`; `x = (Δi − Xi)·kx + w(150) − w(250)` between the pivots and\n`x = (Δa − Xa)·kx + (w_i(150) − w_i(250))·fi + (w_a(150) − w_a(250))·fa` beyond. With the G1 values (`2Ck` = 5 | 7,5,\n`2R1` = 500, `kx` = 1/750, `e0 + Ck/R1` = 0,025 | 0,030, `Ck/R1` = 0,010 | 0,015, `Xi`, `Xa` = 100, 120) these are the\nstandard's formulae; the switching conditions are applied as written.\n\n### 10.5 Motor coaches (A.3.9.2, A.37–A.45)\n\nUnprimed: the motor bogie; primed: the carrying bogie. Each pair gives the larger of its two formulae (NOTE of A.3.9.2).\nDirectional clearances as §10.4: between the pivots `w_i` of both bogies; beyond them `w_i` of the far bogie and `w_a`\nof the bogie at the section's end.\n\n* Between the pivots, `n_μ` = distance from the motor bogie centre (both `n_μ = ni` and `a − ni` are evaluated),\n  `fm = (a − n_μ)/a`, `fc = n_μ/a`, `Δ = a·n_μ − n_μ² + p²/4·fm + p'²/4·fc`:\n  `Ei = max( P + q + w∞·fm + w'∞·fc + z − e0 ;  Δ·k1 + P·fm + q + w(250)·fm + w'(250)·fc + z + [x]>0 − Ck/R1 − e0·fm )`,\n  `x = (Δ − Xi)·kx + (w(150) − w(250))·fm + (w'(150) − w'(250))·fc`.\n* Beyond the pivots, motor bogie at the section's end (A.40–A.42): `Δ = a·n + n² − p²/4·fa + p'²/4·fi`,\n  `Ea = max( (P + q)·A + w∞·fa + w'∞·fi + z − e0 ;  Δ·k1 + P·fa + q·A + w'(250)·fi + w(250)·fa + z + [x]>0 − e0 − Ck/R1 )`.\n* Beyond the pivots, carrying bogie at the section's end (A.43–A.45): `Δ = a·n + n² + p²/4·fi − p'²/4·fa`,\n  `Ea = max( (P + q)·A + w∞·fi + w'∞·fa + z − e0 ;  Δ·k1 + (P + q)·A + w(250)·fi + w'(250)·fa + z + [x]>0 − e0 − Ck/R1 )`.\n* The section's `Ea` is the larger of the two ends. In A.41 the printed `w_i(250)` is read as `w'_i(250)` (the far,\n  carrying bogie), as in A.40 and A.42.\n\n### 10.6 Clearance w varying non-linearly – variable stops (A.3.9.1.2, A.3.9.3.2)\n\nWhen a bogie has variable stops starting at radius `Rv` (with `w(Rv)`), the bracket formulae A.33 / A.35 (traction\nunits) and A.52 / A.54 (coaches) are also evaluated at `R = Rv`:\n`Ei(Rv) = [(Δi − 2Ck)/(2Rv) + w(Rv)] + P + q + z − e0` (coach: `+ q + z`),\n`Ea(Rv) = [(Δa − 2Ck)/(2Rv) + w_i(Rv)·fi + w_a(Rv)·fa] + (P + q)·A + z − e0` (coach: `+ P·fa + q·A + z − e0`)\nwith `w_i(Rv)` = *w_i at that radius* and `w_a(Rv)` = *w_a at that radius* (blank = the inside value),\nand the reduction is the larger of these and §10.4. The 150 m variants (A.34, A.36, A.53, A.55) are not applied: by\nfootnotes 6 and 7 of the standard they do not apply in practice (variable stops act above 250 m); a warning is given\nfor `Rv` below 250 m. Not defined for motor coaches.\n\n`tests/en15273.test.js` and `tests/en15273-mc.test.js` compare the engine with the standard's formulae typed in literally\n(w constant and varying, motor coaches, variable stops).\n\n### 10.7 Heights above the running surface (A.3.4)\n\nChecked for every car type when the EN option is selected; part of the EN verdict.\n\n**Lower parts (A.3.4.1).** One row per part on the Car body & Bogie tab (*Lower parts – EN 15273-2 A.3.4*): car type,\nmounting (car body / bogie frame), position (between / beyond the pivots, or the axles for a bogie part), distance `n`\nfrom the nearer pivot or axle (blank: between = mid, `a/2` or `p/2`; beyond = car-end overhang `n_end` for a body part,\n0 for a bogie part), half-width `b`, height `h` above the rail with the car tare and new wheels, and the lower limit\n`h_lim` at that position (blank = customer minimum clearance, body or bogie; the GIC1 / GIC2 heights of Figures A.5 /\nA.6 at the part's half-width can be entered here). A car type without rows uses the two lower parts of the car body\ninputs (§7.2: body part at `yu` between the pivots at `a/2`, bogie part between the axles).\n\n* Body zones (Figure A.7): between the pivots A (`b ≤ b2`) or B (`b > b2`); beyond them D (`b ≤ b2`) or C (`b > b2`);\n  `b2` = half spacing of the air springs, smaller of the two bogies.\n* Table A.1, locomotives / coaches / motor coaches (no side-bearer clearance, body sag 0), `r = n/a`:\n\n| Zone | Suspension | Wheel wear |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | `Aff` | `Usr` |\n| B | `Aff + 0,1·s·(b − b2)` | `Usr` |\n| C | `Aff + ΔAff·r + 0,1·s·(b − b2)` | `Usr + ΔUsr·r` |\n| D | `Aff + ΔAff·r` | `Usr + ΔUsr·r` |\n\n  `Aff` (car body input; blank = `dp + dsd`, primary + secondary downward travel with the air springs deflated, the\n  total deflection of A.3.4.1.3), `ΔAff`, `s` (zone B / C term; 0 when `Aff` is a bottoming displacement, NOTE of Table\n  A.1) and `ΔUsr` are car body inputs (default 0). `Usr` = wheel radial wear `Δw` of §5.3 (limited by the height\n  correction step `Δr`, as the rest of the tool).\n* Bogie-mounted parts: primary suspension `dp` + `Usr` (the zone terms apply to the body).\n* Gradient transitions (A.3.4.1.6, all vehicles, `Rv ≥ 500 m`), normal values in m, the height required is increased by\n  `[(a·n − n² + p²/4)/1000 − 0,08]>0` between the pivots (A.16) and `[(a·n + n² − p²/4)/1000 − 0,08]>0` beyond them\n  (A.17); `p` = larger wheelbase between, smaller beyond (conservative). For a bogie part `a` is replaced by `p` and the\n  `p²/4` term is 0 (A.3.4.1.5).\n* Check: `h − suspension − wheel wear ≥ h_lim + gradient`; margin = left − right.\n\n**Upper parts (A.3.4.2).** Per section (interior `n = ni` between, exterior `na` and car end `n_end` beyond): profile top\n(empty, no wear: the RS profile as given) `+ 10 mm × suspension levels` (car body input, default 2) `+` A.18 / A.19 (same\nexpressions as A.16 / A.17) `≤` KE top. The vertical quasi-static component is not added (A.3.4.2.2). The standard\napplies this to parts from 3,25 m; a lower profile top is flagged.\n\nNot applied: the hump-shunting terms `dg` of A.3.4.1.5.1 (gravity-shunted vehicles), the wagon terms of Table A.1\n(side-bearer clearance `J·b/bG`, body sag `Flc`), contact ramps (A.3.5), ferries (A.3.7). The GIC1 / GIC2 outlines are\nnot encoded: enter the limit at the part's position.\n\n### 10.8 Bogie parts (A.3.11)\n\nA.3.11: bogies and their parts use the reduction formulae of vehicles with independent axles (A.3.10.1, w = 0), the\nbogie being the vehicle and its end axles the \"pivots\". One row per part on the Car body & Bogie tab (*Bogie parts –\nEN 15273-2 A.3.11*): bogie type, mounting (bogie frame / axle or axlebox), position (between / beyond the end axles),\ndistance `n` from the nearer end axle (blank: between `p/2`, beyond 0), half-width `y` and height `h` (tare, new wheels).\n\nWith `a = p` (distance between the end axles), `P = (l_max − d)/2` (`d` = BB + 2·ef worn of that bogie type),\n`q` primary lateral play, `k1 = 1/(2·R1)`, `Ck` of the lower parts for `h ≤ h_low`, else of the upper parts:\n\n| | condition for the straight branch | straight branch | curve branch |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Between, A.56 / A.57 | `Δi = a·n − n² ≤ 2Ck` | `P + q + z − e0` | `Δi·k1 + P + q + z − e0 − Ck/R1` |\n| Beyond, A.58 / A.59 | `Δa = a·n + n² ≤ 2Ck` | `(P + q)·A + z − e0` | `Δa·k1 + (P + q)·A + z − e0 − Ck/R1` |\n\n`A = (2n + a)/a`. In A.56 the printed condition `a·n_a + n_a²` is read as `a·n_i − n_i²` (as in A.57). With the G1\nvalues (`2Ck` = 5 | 7,5, `e0` = 0,015, `e0 + Ck/R1` = 0,025 | 0,030) these are the standard's formulae; for bogies\n`Δ` is small and the straight branches (= A.27 / A.30 with w = 0) apply, as the standard notes.\n\n* `z` (A.24 with `C0 = I0` between, `D0` beyond): frame-mounted parts roll on the primary suspension with the bogie\n  input `s` and `hc` of the frame (default 0 = no roll); axle-mounted parts are unsprung, `z = 0` (A.3.8.1.1). No\n  dissymmetry term.\n* Reference: the customer KE over its whole outline (lower notch included). The part moves vertically between\n  `h − Δw − dp` and `h + up` (frame) or `h − Δw` and `h` (axle), `Δw` = wheel radial wear limited by `Δr`; the KE\n  half-width used is the smallest over that range (at its ends and at every KE vertex inside).\n* Margin = KE half-width − E − y; every bogie part must have margin ≥ 0 (in the EN verdict and the overall static\n  verdict). The heights of bogie-mounted parts are checked in §10.7 (lower parts table, mounting *bogie frame*).\n* Not applied: the 5 mm reduction of z for two-axle wagons (A.3.10.1, wagons only).\n\n### 10.9 Static gauge method (A.2)\n\nOptional (*Also check EN 15273-2:2009 static gauge method*, Static Simulation tab), independent of the kinematic check.\nThe reference profile is a profile on the Customer KE & SOD tab (option; blank = the KE of the static check), read like\na KE (upper envelope to `n_up`). Rules (Customer KE & SOD tab, G1 values): allowance `0,075 m`, minimum `Δ = 7,5 m²`,\n`s_max = 0,2` (§5.1.3: 0,3 for GA, GB, GC), lower parts up to `0,43 m`; `l_max`, `R1`, `R2`, `Xi`, `Xa` as §10.1.\n\n* A.2.3.1: the static gauge may be used only when the flexibility coefficient does not exceed `s_max`; `s` = largest of\n  the values used for the load cases (§5.4 source and lateral state). Otherwise the car type fails the static-gauge check\n  (\"use the kinematic or dynamic method\").\n* Reductions, `P = (l_max − d)/2`, `w` = secondary lateral play used (§5.2), `q` primary, `k1 = 1/(2·R1)`,\n  `kx = 1/(2·R2) − 1/(2·R1)`:\n  * between the pivots (A.2, A.3): `Δi = max(a·n − n² + p²/4 ; Δmin)`, `x = (a·n − n² + p²/4 − Xi)·kx`,\n    `Ei = [Δi·k1 + P + q + w + [x]>0 − allowance]>0`;\n  * beyond them (A.4, A.5): `Δa = max(a·n + n² − p²/4 ; Δmin)`, `x = (a·n + n² − p²/4 − Xa)·kx`,\n    `Ea = [Δa·k1 + (P + q + w)·(2n + a)/a + [x]>0 − allowance]>0`.\n  With the G1 values (`2R1` = 500, `kx` = 1/750, 7,5, 100, 120, 0,075) these are A.2–A.5. `p` = larger wheelbase\n  between the pivots, smaller beyond (conservative); `d` from the bogie with the larger play. No roll term: the static\n  gauge contains no quasi-static allowance and is limited by `s_max` instead.\n* Check of the RS profiles: at every profile vertex height and every reference vertex height within the profile,\n  margin = `W_ref(h) − E − x_prof(h)` (reference half-width at `max(h, bottom of the upper envelope)`); the profile top\n  shall not exceed the reference top.\n* A.2.3.2 lower parts (rows of §10.7 with height ≤ 0,43 m): `h − Usr − (dp + dsd)` (bogie parts `h − Usr − dp`, total\n  bottoming displacement) `≥` lower limit (entered, or the customer minimum). The GIS1 / GIS2 outlines are not encoded.\n* The car type passes when s ≤ s_max, every margin ≥ 0, the tops fit and the lower parts are above their limits; the\n  result is part of the verdict when the option is selected. Tests: `tests/en15273-static.test.js` (A.2–A.5 typed in).\n\n### 10.10 Open doors and deployed steps (A.3.14, A.3.15)\n\nPart of the kinematic check (§10.1–10.8). One row per element on the RS Profile tab (*Open doors and deployed steps*):\ncar type, kind (access door open / footboard or step deployed), position (between / beyond the pivots), distance `n`\nfrom the nearer pivot (blank: between `a/2`, beyond the car-end overhang), half-width `y` of the open element and its\nlowest / highest point with the car tare and new wheels. Valid for speeds up to 5 km/h.\n\n* Lowest position of the stationary vehicle: both heights are lowered by the total downward movement `Σd` of §5.3\n  (suspension state of the static options).\n* Heights checked: the two ends, the boundary `h_d = 1,05 m` for doors, and every KE vertex in between.\n* Maximum construction gauge at the element: `W_KE(h) − E(h)` with `E = Ei` (between) or `Ea` (beyond) of §10.4–10.6 at\n  the element's own `n`, worst of the load states and bogie orientations.\n* Allowance added to it (rules on the Customer KE & SOD tab, G1 defaults):\n\n| Element | Height | Between the pivots | Beyond the pivots |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Door, a) | `h ≥ 1,05 m` | `0,2 m` | `0,2 m` |\n| Door, b) | `h < 1,05 m` | `min(w_i ; 0,035 m)` | `w_i·n/a + (w_a − X)>0·(n + a)/a` (A.105), `X = 0,02 m` |\n| Step | any | `min(w_i ; 0,035 m)` | `w_i·n/a + w_a·(n + a)/a` (A.106) |\n\n  `w` = straight-track secondary clearance of each bogie (`w_i` far bogie, `w_a` bogie at the element's end); both\n  orientations are evaluated and the smaller allowance is used (between the pivots: the smaller `w`).\n* Check: `y ≤ W_KE(h) − E(h) + allowance` at every height; margin = right − left.\n* Step position (A.3.15.2), steps only, when a verification radius `R` and a platform position `b_q0i` (inside of the\n  curve) and / or `b_q0a` (outside) are given; gap limit `b_gap0` = customer PRM gap. Geometric overthrow at the step\n  `dg = (a·n − n² + p²/4)/(2R)` between (larger `p`), `(a·n + n² − p²/4)/(2R)` beyond (smaller `p`). The mid-car moves\n  inwards and the car ends outwards, so the gap is `b_q0 − b ∓ dg` (− when the step moves towards the platform). The gap\n  varies linearly with `1/R`, so its largest value between straight track and `R` is the larger of `b_q0 − b` and the\n  value at `R`; the check is `gap_max ≤ b_gap0`. For the side where the step moves away from the platform this is the\n  standard's `dg ≤ dg_max` (`dg_imax = b + b_gap0 − b_q0a`, `dg_amax = b + b_gap0 − b_q0i`); for the other side the\n  standard's `dg_max` expressions (`b_q0i − b − b_gap0`, `b_q0a − b − b_gap0`) are shown for information – there the\n  gap is largest on straight track.\n* A failed element fails the EN verdict. Not applied: the opening movement of doors fulfilling a) and b) together\n  (to be checked on drawings), A.3.15.3 platform-mounted devices.\n\n### 10.11 Pantograph gauge (A.3.12)\n\nPart of the kinematic check, for overhead-line vehicles. One row per pantograph on the RS Profile tab: car type,\nposition (between / beyond the pivots), `n` from the nearer pivot (blank: `a/2` or the car-end overhang), height of the\npantograph base `h_t` (lower articulation; the head's lateral flexibility is taken as growing linearly from `h_t`, as in\nA.68), flexibility `t` (lateral head displacement at 6,5 m under 300 N), inclination tolerance `θ` (rad), construction /\ninstallation tolerance `τ` (at 6,5 m), head semi-width `b_w`, and optionally the top of the lowered pantograph and the\ninsulating distance. Everything in m in the formulae; the numbers are those of G1 (fixed).\n\n`s ≤ 0,225` (A.66–A.70, A.76–A.80): `k = 0,066`, `c' = 0,1175`, `c\" = 0,0925`; `s > 0,225` (A.71–A.75, A.81–A.85):\n`k = 0,2`, `c' = 0,2375`, `c\" = 0,1825`:\n\n* `z' = k·(6,5 − hc)/1,5·s + √(t² + τ² + [θ·(6,5 − hc)]²) − c'`\n* `z\" = k·(5 − hc)/1,5·s + √([t·(5 − h_t)/(6,5 − h_t)]² + τ² + [θ·(5 − hc)]²) − c\"`\n* between the pivots (vehicle stationary on canted track): `j'_i = q + w_iR − 0,0375`,\n  `Po = [(a·n − n² + p²/4 − 5)/(2R)]>0 + j'_i + z'`, `Pu` the same with `z\"`;\n* beyond the pivots (running with cant deficiency): `j'_a = q·(2n + a)/a + w_aR·(n + a)/a + w_iR·n/a − 0,0375`,\n  `Po = [(a·n + n² − p²/4 − 5)/(2R)]>0 + (1,465 − d)/2·2n/a + j'_a + z'`, `Pu` with `z\"`.\n\nThe pantograph is inside the gauge when `Po ≤ 0` (upper collecting point, 6,5 m) and `Pu ≤ 0` (lower, 5,0 m). Cases\n(A.3.12.3): straight track (`R = ∞`, geometric term 0), `R = R2` (150 m) and, for a bogie with variable stops, the\nradius `Rv` where they begin. `w_R` of each bogie at `R`: `w∞`, `w(Rv)` at `Rv`, otherwise linear in `1/R` through\n`w∞`, `w(250)`, `w(150)`. `s`, `hc` of every load case (§10.2), both bogie orientations, larger `p` between and smaller\nbeyond; the worst case is reported, every case is exported.\n\nLowered pantograph (A.3.12.7), when its top is given: `b_w + insulating distance ≤ W_KE(h) − E(h)` at the pantograph's\n`n`, and `h + insulating distance ≤` KE top. Not covered: the special zones and the 25 kV live-part zone of Figure A.16\nas data (A.3.12.2, A.3.12.8), tilting vehicles and `I_p > I_c` (A.3.13).\n\n### 10.12 Other reference gauges (Annexes B, C, E, L)\n\n*Reference gauge of the associated rules* (Customer KE & SOD tab) loads the rules of a gauge into the rule fields; the\nreference profile of that gauge is entered by the user as a KE profile (the profiles of the standard are not bundled).\nValues can be edited afterwards (a warning lists the differences; *Custom* for own rules).\n\n* **GC, G2, DE3**: the rules of G1 (B.3.3.2, B.3.5.2, E.1.1.1, E.1.2.1, L.3.2); GC static `s_max = 0,3`.\n* **GA, GB, GB1, GB2** – height law (B.2.2, B.3, Tables B.1–B.4, C.1, C.2): `k = (h − h_0)/span`, `0 ≤ k ≤ 1`, with\n  `h_0 = 3,25 m` (kinematic) / `3,22 m` (static) and span 0,63 / 0,86 / 0,96 / 1,10 m (GA / GB / GB1 / GB2). Above\n  `h_0`, for the upper parts:\n  * kinematic: `2·Ck = 7,5 + 32,5·k` (so `e0 + Ck/R1 = 0,030 + 0,065·k`, `Ck/R1 = 0,015 + 0,065·k`, and the coach\n    condition `250·(l_max − d) + 32,5·k`), `Xa = 120 − 20·k` (`Xi = 100` unchanged), `s0 = 0,4 − 0,1·k` in z_kin\n    (`(0,04 − 0,01·k)·(h − 0,5)` of Tables B.2–B.4). These are formulae B.5–B.10 (traction units), B.21–B.26 (coaches)\n    and the same generalisation of §10.4; the non-linear w formulae B.11–B.14 / B.27–B.30 follow §10.6 with this `Ck`.\n    Motor coaches with one motor bogie use A.3.9.2 as it is (B.3.4.1, `k = 0`); the alternative B.15–B.20 is not used.\n  * static: `Δmin = 7,5 + 32,5·k`, allowance `0,075 + 0,065·k` (B.1, B.3), and `Xa = 100` in B.4 (as printed).\n  * static `s_max = 0,3` (B.2, §5.1.3). The pantograph rules stay those of G1 (B.1 c).\n* **DE2** (L.2.2): G2 (= G1) rules, and between 3,765 m and 4,335 m the roll term uses `s0 = 0,19`, `hc0 = 0,695 m` and\n  `k2 = (I or D − C0 + 0,015 + 0,039)/L` with `I or D` the line's maximum (example 0,150 m), i.e.\n  `Δz = s/L·[I or D − 0,05 + 0,054]·(h − hc) − 0,19/L·[…]·(h − 0,695)` added as the bracket of A.24.\n* **DE1** (L.1.2): the same roll rule over the full height with `s0 = 0,28`, `hc0 = 0,70 m`. The radius corrections\n  `Δs` of L.1.2.2 (≤ 0 for 150 m ≤ R ≤ 500 m) are not applied, and the reductions use the G1 formula structure with\n  the reference radius 250 m; a warning says to confirm with the infrastructure manager.\n\nImplementation: `enRulesAt(r, h)` (kinematic) and `enStaticRulesAt(r, h)` (static) return the rules at height `h`; the\nreductions of §10.4–10.11 use them at every height. `tests/en15273-gauges.test.js` types in B.1–B.10, B.21–B.26 and the\nDE2 roll term literally.\n\n### 10.13 Results\n\n* Maximum construction gauge on the Method A heights (§7): `W_KE(h) − E(h)` per section, shown with the workbook\n  Method A for comparison.\n* Check of the RS profiles: at every profile vertex height and every KE vertex height within the profile height range,\n  margin = `W_KE(h) − E(h) − x_prof(h)` (KE half-width at `max(h, bottom of the upper envelope)`, as §6). The car type\n  passes when every margin ≥ 0; when the option is selected, the EN result is part of the car's verdict.\n* Heights (§10.7): one row per lower part and per section, in the EN verdict.\n* Bogie parts (§10.8): one row per part and bogie type.\n* Static gauge (§10.9): per car type, when selected.\n* Doors and steps (§10.10): one row per element and governing height; step position per platform side.\n* Pantographs (§10.11): worst Po and Pu per pantograph, lowered position.\n* Not covered yet: the GIC1/GIC2 and GIS1/GIS2 outlines as data (limits are entered), the reference-profile outlines of the other gauges (entered as KE profiles), the gauges of Annexes D, F–K, M, N (the dynamic method of §5.2 / Annex O is §15); tilting vehicles (A.3.16)\n  and wagons (A.3.10) are out of scope. Articulated units use the Figure 1\n  equivalence; the formulae assume two-axle bogies (§5.1.2) – warnings are given otherwise.\n\n## 11. Assumptions and limitations\n\n1. Quasi-static kinematic method: all terms added linearly unless the RSS option is selected. Dynamic\n   movements beyond the bump-stop values (w) need multibody simulation and are not modelled.\n2. The customer KE is a tangent-track, track-plane envelope; curve effects and lean due to cant belong to the\n   structure gauge (SOD practice).\n3. Only the lean side of each section is checked (x ≥ 0); the rising side moves inwards and is normally not\n   governing — check tall, wide roof equipment separately.\n4. Clearance from horizontal and vertical distances (§6.4): exact for a straight KE segment, conservative near\n   corners.\n5. Theoretical s and hc use a two-stage roll model; the sway test governs. Inter-car roll coupling of shared\n   bogies is ignored; an identical neighbour body is assumed for shared-bogie loads.\n6. The throw formulas are valid for two-bogie bodies and R ≥ about 150 m; below that use the graphic or\n   swept-path method.\n7. Default options reproduce the reference method choices: no gauge tolerance on exterior / car end, no\n   alignment tolerance, upward movement on the centre line only.\n8. A car with two different bogie types uses the larger play, travel and wheelbase of the two.\n\n## 12. Out of scope\n\nPantograph sway and flexible-catenary checks (the rigid-catenary clearance is §5.3e), multibody dynamic simulation, coupled-train (rescue) gauging, and curves below\n150 m by the graphic method. (The sway-test evaluation and the kinematic gauge test plan are in §13.)\n\n## 13. Test calculations (Tests tab)\n\n### 13.1 Sway-test evaluation\n\nThe vehicle stands on straight, level track; shims of height `D` are put under the wheels of one side (cant `D` over\nthe rail-centre distance `L`). Each test row gives: car type, load state (tare, crush, deflated), `D`, the low side, and\nthe lateral displacement `y` of the body at two or three heights `h` above the rail — measured in the track plane\n(perpendicular to the plane of the shimmed rails), relative to the reading at `D = 0` of the same load state, positive\ntowards the low rail.\n\nModel (as §5): `y = s·(D/L)·(h − hc)`, i.e. `y = a·(D·h) + b·D` with `a = s/L`, `b = −s·hc/L`. For each car type and load\nstate, `a` and `b` are fitted by least squares over all readings (no intercept: the zero-cant reading is the reference):\n\n`[Σu² Σuv; Σuv Σv²]·[a; b] = [Σu·y; Σv·y]`, `u = D·h`, `v = D`  →  `s = a·L`, `hc = −b/a`\n\nwith the RMS residual. At least two different heights are needed. When both sides were tested, `s` is also fitted per\nside (the difference shows suspension asymmetry).\n\nAcceptance (owner's choice: the roll displacement decides): the roll displacement per 100 mm of cant,\n`m(h) = 100·s·(h − hc)/L`, measured ≤ the value used in gauging at `h1`, `h2` (Customer KE & SOD tab) and at the top of\nthe car type's RS profiles. Values used: `s`, `hc` of that load state from the source chosen for gauging (sway-test\ninputs on the Car body tab or the theoretical values, §5.4).\n\n### 13.2 Kinematic gauge test plan\n\nFor each car type and static case of the tare and crush load cases (§6, needs a static run; cases of the role\n*other* are not tested), the case is reproduced on straight test track, and every\nterm of the static displacement is either **reproduced** on the test stand or **taken off the KE** to form the test\ntemplate (owner's choice: plays and shims are reproduced):\n\n* Reproduced — shims: `D_test = cant + D_w` (cant excess for the interior, deficiency for the exterior and car end, plus\n  the wind equivalent cant) under the low side, giving the roll `θq·(h − hc)` through the vehicle's own suspension.\n* Reproduced — lateral, towards the low side: wheel–rail play, primary `q` and secondary `w`, each × `A` of the section\n  (interior: both bogies pushed the same way; exterior / car end: bogies pushed in opposite directions to yaw the body),\n  total `R_lat = (play + q + w)·A`.\n* Taken off the KE: geometric overthrow at `Rs`, rail gauge-face wear, gauge widening, the random terms (gauge and\n  alignment tolerance, cross-level roll and rigid tilt, linear or RSS as selected) and, vertically, the upward movement\n  and the vertical shift of the random roll.\n\nFor each profile point with static `(x0, h0)` and the static run's displaced point `(x, h)`, KE width `W(h)` and KE height\n`H(x)`:\n\n| Quantity | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Expected position on the test | `x_t = x0 + R_lat + θq·(h0 − hc)`, `h_t = h0 − θq·x0 − drop` |\n| Lateral terms not reproduced | `N = E(h0) − R_lat − θq·(h0 − hc)` (E of §6.2) |\n| Vertical terms not reproduced | `V = up − (θr + θt)·x0` |\n| Test template | half-width `W_t = W(h) − N` at height `h_t`; height `H_t = H(x) − V` |\n| Expected margins | `W_t − x_t = ΔX`, `H_t − h_t = ΔH`: equal to the static run (§6.4), so the plan is consistent with Method B |\n\nPositions are measured perpendicular to the shimmed rail plane, as in the sway test. Crush cases need the crush load on\nthe vehicle (body lowering `drop`). When a sway test of the car type and load state is entered, the expected position\nis also given with the measured `s`, `hc`: `x_m = x0 + R_lat + s_m·(D_test/L)·(h0 − hc_m)`, `h_m = h0 − s_m·(D_test/L)·x0 − drop`,\nand its clearance to the template (§6.4); when `D_test` exceeds the largest shim of that sway test, the linear roll\nmodel is used beyond the tested range and the plan says so. Note: a stiffer vehicle than assumed (smaller roll) moves less laterally but\nits roof corners on the low side drop less (`h − θ·x`), so the clearance with the measured values can be smaller near the\nKE top even when the sway-test acceptance of §13.1 is met; the plan shows both clearances.\n\n\n### 13.3 Static gauge measurement of a built car\n\nMeasured points of a car (gauge template or laser scanner): car type, section (interior / exterior / car end), point\nname, half-width Y and height Z above the rail. After a static run, each point is treated as a profile point of its\nsection (§6.3–§6.4): moved by every movement of every case of that section, the smallest clearance to the customer KE\nand its case are given; the car section passes when every point is inside. Deviation = measured Y − design half-width\nof the section's profile of the run at the same height (+ = wider than designed; blank above the profile top).\n\n## 14. Third-rail current collector (CCD)\n\nNot part of EN 15273-2 (which covers pantographs only); metro practice from the customer KE and the conductor-rail\ninterface data. Checked whenever a CCD is given, in the static run and along the line; part of both verdicts.\n\n### 14.1 Inputs\n\n* **CCD** (Car body & Bogie tab), per CCD type: bogie type that carries it, mounting (bogie frame, primary sprung /\n  axleboxes, unsprung), distance `x` of the shoe from the bogie centre (beyond the end axle when `x > p/2`), working\n  range `[c_min, c_max]` of the shoe on the contact axis (positions at which it still presses on the rail), extent\n  `[o_min, o_max]` of its contact face on the other axis.\n* **CCD outline points**: per CCD, points in the working and the retracted position, rail side (`y` from the track\n  centre, `z` above rail, tare and new wheels); part *shoe* or *gear*.\n* **Conductor rail** (Customer KE & SOD tab): contact type — bottom (`z` contact axis, shoe pushes up), top (`z`, shoe\n  pushes down) or side (`y`, shoe pushes outwards); contact surface `c` (height, or distance from the track centre for\n  side contact); face extent `[r_min, r_max]` on the other axis; installation tolerances `±t_y`, `±t_z`; wear `w_r` of\n  the contact face (it moves the face away from the shoe); minimum face overlap; envelope for the check (a profile on the\n  tab; blank = the KE of the static check / of the track segment). Assembly points (brackets, shroud / cover board,\n  insulators) with a required clearance each.\n* Rail sides along the line: SOD point items of type *Third rail* (side and chainage range); without such items the\n  rail is taken on both sides everywhere.\n\n### 14.2 Movements of a CCD point (mm, `+y` towards the rail)\n\nFor a point at `(y, z)` of a CCD on bogie `b` (`p` wheelbase), in a situation with local curvature `k`, the rail on the\noutside or inside of the curve, cant deficiency / excess `D_t` leaning the bogie towards the rail and `D_a` away from it:\n\n* random lateral: `ρ = (l − d)/2 + q (frame) + f_w + w_d/2 (R < R_w) + Δl (option) + al (option)` (as §5.2);\n* geometric offset of the point from the track centre: `O = (x² − p²/4)/2·k`, outwards; towards the rail when the rail\n  is on the outside, away from it when inside;\n* frame roll on the primary suspension (bogie inputs `s_f`, `h_cf`, frame-mounted only): `θ_t = s_f·D_t/L`,\n  `θ_a = s_f·D_a/L`; lateral `θ_t·(z − h_cf)` and `−θ_a·(z − h_cf)`;\n* lateral range: `[O − ρ + min(0, θ_t·(z − h_cf), −θ_a·(z − h_cf)), O + ρ + max(…)]`;\n* vertical range: `[−Δw − dp (frame) − θ_t·y + v_lo, up (frame) + θ_a·y + v_hi]`, `Δw` = wheel radial wear limited by\n  `Δr`, `dp` / `up` primary downward / upward travel; vertical curve `δ = (x² − p²/4)/(2·Rv)` (crest positive):\n  static `v = ±|δ|` with `Rv` of the static check, along the line `v = [min(0, δ), max(0, δ)]` with the segment's `Rv`.\n\nA point is moved to the rectangle `[y + y_lo, y + y_hi] × [z + z_lo, z + z_hi]`. A working shoe point follows the rail\non the contact axis instead: its coordinate there spans the rail range `[c − t − (w_r if the face wears towards −),\nc + t + (w_r if it wears towards +)] − c`.\n\n### 14.3 Checks\n\n1. **Envelope**: every point except the working shoe (which touches the rail by design) — all four corners of its\n   rectangle inside the envelope (right half closed along the centre line); margin = smallest signed distance.\n2. **Reach** (contact axis): rail position relative to the shoe frame `[c_lo − s_hi, c_hi − s_lo]` (`s` = movement of\n   the shoe reference point `((o_min + o_max)/2, c)` on that axis) within `[c_min, c_max]`; margin = smaller of the\n   two ends.\n3. **Overlap** (other axis): with the shoe face shifted by `δ ∈ [s'_lo − t', s'_hi + t']`,\n   `overlap = min(o_max + δ, r_max) − max(o_min + δ, r_min)`, smallest at the ends of `δ`; margin = overlap − minimum.\n4. **Clearance**: distance between each moved CCD rectangle (working and retracted) and each assembly point widened by\n   the rail tolerances, minus its required clearance.\n\nStatic situations: tangent; minimum radius `R_min` (Track Data) with the rail outside (`D_t` = max cant deficiency,\n`D_a` = max cant excess) and inside (reversed); vertical curve both ways in each. Along the line: every chainage of the\nsweep with a conductor rail, its local curvature, deficiency at line speed, excess and `Rv`, each rail side present;\nthe curve frame of §8.7 decides whether that side is outside (`k > 0`: right side outside). The worst CCD and side per\nchainage is reported.\n\nNot covered: shoe dynamics (bounce, contact force), gap bridging and ramps, the shoe fuse and arcing distances other\nthan the clearance points given, articulated or steered bogies.\n\n## 15. EN 15273-2 dynamic method (EN Dynamic Method tab)\n\nEN 15273-2:2009 §5.2 with the recommended practice of Annex O (informative). The vehicle movements come from a\nvalidated multibody simulation run outside this tool; the tool post-processes them, checks the swept envelope and checks\nthe model validation. It does not simulate.\n\n### 15.1 Inputs\n\n* **Datum points per car type**: the outer datums at `±x_d` from the body centre (blank = `a/2`, over the bogie\n  centres), the centre datum at 0, the datum height `h_d` (roll is applied about it), and the quasi-static drop terms\n  `f_z`, `f_cr`, `f_w`, `f_tol` (O.3.8.4): `f_max = f_z + f_cr + f_w + f_tol` (O.10).\n* **Transient results** (O.3.8): per car type, condition (tare, crush, deflated, …), speed `v` and cant-excess case `I`,\n  and datum (lead, centre, trail): mean (absolute) and standard deviation of the transverse displacement `TH_y`, the\n  vertical displacement `TH_z` (mm) and the roll angle `TH_θ` (mrad). A missing centre datum is the average of the outer\n  ones (O.1–O.3).\n* **Curving results** (O.3.9): per car type, condition and radius `R`: quasi-static displacement towards the outside and\n  the inside at the leading and trailing datums, without the geometric overthrow.\n* **Annex O.6 spreadsheet import**: counts in A2 (speeds), B2 (cants), C2 (radii), D2 (vehicles); speeds in m/s from\n  A4 (converted to km/h), cants from B4, radii from C4; transient blocks of 3 rows (lead, centre, trail) per speed × cant\n  case from row `N = max(counts) + 5`, 6 columns per vehicle from column C (transverse mean, σ, vertical mean, σ, roll\n  mean, σ); curving rows from row `N + N_v·N_c·3 + 2`, radius in column A and 4 columns per vehicle from B (outside\n  lead, outside trail, inside lead, inside trail). Vehicle `k` is the `k`-th car type of the Train Configuration. Units\n  and the condition name are settings.\n\n### 15.2 Displacements (O.4–O.18)\n\nWith `k_σ = 3/√2` (setting):\n\n* cant-deficiency case `D = c` from the analysed excess case `I = c` (O.4–O.9): `TH_y`, `TH_θ` and all `σ` unchanged,\n  `TH_z(D) = 2·TH_z(0) − TH_z(I)` with `TH_z(0)` the zero-cant case at the same speed (if missing, `TH_z(I)` is used and\n  flagged);\n* transverse `Dpl = TH_y + k_σ·σ_y` (O.11, O.12); downwards `Δh_d = TH_z + k_σ·σ_z + f_max` (O.13, O.14); upwards\n  `Δh_u = TH_z + k_σ·σ_z` (O.15, O.16); roll `θ = TH_θ + k_σ·σ_θ` (O.17, O.18).\n\n### 15.3 Swept envelope (rigid body)\n\nA body section at longitudinal position `x` (interior `a/2 − ni`, exterior `a/2 + na`, car end `a/2 + n_end`; each at\n`+x`, leading, and `−x`, trailing) is moved as a rigid body: `Dpl`, `Δh_d`, `Δh_u` and `θ` are interpolated linearly\nalong the body from the three datums (lead at `+x_d`, centre 0, trail at `−x_d`) and extrapolated beyond them. A profile\npoint `(y, h)` on the side the vehicle leans to moves to\n\n* `y' = y + Dpl(x) + Y_curve(R, x) + O(x) + (h − h_d)·θ(x)`,\n* `h' = h − y·θ(x) − Δh_d(x)` and `h' = h − y·θ(x) + Δh_u(x)` (both are checked),\n\nwith the geometric offset of the body centreline `O(x) = (x² − a²/4 − p²/4)/(2R)` (outwards positive; inwards at mid-car,\noutwards beyond the pivots) and `Y_curve` from the curving results, linear in `1/R` between the analysed radii (0 on\nstraight track, the smallest radius's value below it, flagged), the centre being the average of lead and trail.\n\n* **Tangent check**: every condition and case, every section at `±x`, `R = ∞`, leaning outwards; margin = signed distance\n  to the whole reference profile (right half mirrored; setting, blank = the KE of the static check).\n* **Along the line** (with the dynamic simulation, option): at each chainage with an SOD, the outside (right in the curve\n  frame) uses the deficiency case with the smallest analysed speed `≥ V` and cant `≥` the deficiency at line speed, the\n  inside the excess case with the smallest speed and cant `≥` the applied cant (stopped train); beyond the analysed cases\n  the largest is used and the point is flagged. `R` and the overthrow use the governing curvature of §8. The swept points\n  are checked against the SOD outline widened by the curve allowance and rotated as in §8.7, with the same required\n  clearance; the result is part of the dynamic verdict.\n\n### 15.4 Model validation (O.2)\n\nPer car type and condition: weighbridge (O.2.4) — percentage errors of the wheel, wheelset and side loads, maximum and\naverage against Table O.1 (15/7, 6/3, 3/3 %); bogie rotation (O.2.5) — maximum X factor, 15/7 % (Table O.2); sway test\n(O.2.6) — absolute error of the four-part averages per output, maximum against 4 / 4 mrad, 10 / 10 mm, and the average\nhysteresis-loop error (entered) against 2 / 2 mrad, 15 / 10 mm (Table O.3; primary roll, secondary roll, solebar sway,\nsolebar drop); dynamic ride test (O.2.7) — matched or not, judged by a competent person. Status (Figure O.1):\nweighbridge and sway matched → *statically validated* (quasi-static use only); in addition bogie rotation and ride test\nmatched → *fully validated*; statically validated with notified-body acceptance → *partially validated*; otherwise\n*not validated*. Percentage errors use the measured value as the base.\n\nNot covered: the multibody simulation itself, the comparator-vehicle method (Table O.5), pantograph swept envelopes,\nand PSD comparison of the ride test (entered as a judgement). `tests/en-dynamic.test.js`.\n\n## 16. Applicable rules (framework)\n\n### 16.1 Selection\n\n*Applicable rules* on the Train Configuration tab chooses the framework of the project; it loads the default checks\ninto the Static Simulation options (and, for EN and EBO, the EN rules of the reference gauge). Every check can then be\nswitched individually on the Static Simulation tab; the *Applicable rules* table there shows the framework default and\nthe current choice (marked *changed* when they differ).\n\n| Framework | Customer KE (Method A/B) | EN kinematic | EN static gauge | BOStrab §34 | Rules loaded |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Customer KE – metro practice | yes | no | no | no | – |\n| EN 15273-2 | yes | yes | no | no | gauge G1 |\n| EBO – German main line | yes | yes | no | no | G1 (§22, cross-border) or G2 (other vehicles) |\n| BOStrab – German tram, light rail, U-Bahn | yes | no | no | yes | – |\n\nThe third-rail CCD (§14) and the EN dynamic method (§15) run whenever their data are given.\n\n### 16.2 EBO (pending documents)\n\nEBO §22: G1 (Anlage 7) for vehicles in cross-border traffic, G2 (Anlage 8) for the others, reduced by Anlage 9. The tool\nuses the EN 15273-2 kinematic rules of gauge G1 or G2 (§10), whose terms match the effects listed in Anlage 9 (plays,\nwear, vertical oscillation and curves, tilt for 50 mm cant excess / deficiency, dissymmetry beyond 1°). Not yet\nincluded, pending the documents: the reduction formula of Anlage 9 item 2, its track gauge of 1,435 m for bogie\nvehicles on curves (1,465 m otherwise), the lower-part reference lines of Anlagen 7 / 8. A warning is given.\n\n### 16.3 BOStrab §34 (public limits)\n\nWith *Also check BOStrab §34 vehicle dimensions*, per car type (limits editable, defaults of the regulation):\n\n* §34(3), street-running vehicles (*straßenabhängig*, Train Configuration): largest width `2·x` of the RS profiles up to\n  the split height 3,4 m (including the width at that height) ≤ 2,65 m; above it ≤ 2,25 m; height to the top of the\n  lowered pantograph (pantograph table) or, without one, the RS profile top ≤ 4,0 m.\n* §34(2), street track: extra curve throw against straight track at the minimum radius `R_min` (Track Data), inside at\n  mid-car `(a² + p²)/(8R)` and outside at the car end `(a·n_end + n_end² − p²/4)/(2R)`, each ≤ 0,65 m.\n* For vehicles that are not street-running the checks are shown as not applicable.\n\nNot yet included, pending the documents: the Lichtraumbedarf method of the BOStrab-Lichtraum-Richtlinien (1996), with\nits statistical combination (§18(3); the RSS option of §6 can be used meanwhile) and safety distance (§18(4)). A warning\nis given. `tests/framework.test.js`.\n";
